# Workflow

How work moves through this repository, and why. The step-by-step procedures are skills (`.agents/skills/`, indexed in [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md)); this file holds the conventions and the reasons behind them.

## The two gates

Work is split so that a human decides twice:

1. **Plan gate.** The plan is posted on the issue and nothing is edited until the human approves it. A wrong direction is cheap to correct in a comment and expensive in a diff.
2. **Merge gate.** The agent opens a PR and stops. The human reviews and merges.

The gates are also the points where the human can switch models or hand the PR to a separate reviewing agent.

### Enforcement

The merge gate should not depend on the agent's cooperation. Set up on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %}:

- **Agents use their own account or token without merge rights** on the repository (write access to branches and PRs, no permission to merge into `main`).
- **Branch protection on `main`:** no direct pushes, merge only with an approving review from a human.
- **Backstops in the repo:** {% if hooks != 'none' %}the `no-commit-to-main` pre-commit hook blocks local commits on `main`, and {% endif %}CI runs `just check` on every push and pull request.

There is no merge recipe in the `justfile`. The plan gate stays a convention; the `plan-issue` skill carries it out.

## Where information lives

| Place | What belongs there |
|---|---|
| `docs/` | What should outlive the task: architecture, domain rules, conventions, runbooks, ADRs |
| Issue | The problem, the plan, decisions made along the way |
| PR body | The handoff: what changed, how it was verified, assumptions, open questions, ADRs |
| `.ai/` | Scratch: body files for `just`, notes, throwaway scripts. Gitignored, never referenced from committed files, never the only copy of something that matters |

Issues and PRs on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %} are the authoritative record of planned and completed work, so a new session, another machine or another tool can pick up from them.
{%- if platform == 'both' %} GitHub is a mirror: the code is readable there, but issues, PRs and releases live on Forgejo.
{%- endif %}

When you find a bug, open an issue before or alongside the fix. Nothing gets fixed without a record.

**Drift policy.** If a change makes a doc wrong, fix the doc in the same PR. Move only stable conclusions into `docs/`; task chatter stays in the issue. Put a finding into the existing file it belongs to; create a new `docs/domain/` file only for a subject that has no home. Write an ADR for every non-obvious architectural decision.

## Conventions

**Branches:** `<type>/<short-description>`, always from an up-to-date `main`. Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`.

**Commits:** conventional commits, see [codestyle.md](codestyle.md#commit-messages). Release notes are generated from them.

**Issue titles:** plain English and problem-focused — what is wrong or needed, not what will be done. No type prefix, no solution in the title.

**PRs:** reference the issue (`Closes #N` or `Part of #N`). The body follows the template in the `implement-issue` skill.

**Forge operations:** through `just` only; titles are arguments, bodies are files written to `.ai/` first. `just --list` shows what exists; the `forge` skill covers the rest.

## Quality gates

```bash
{% if python == 'component' %}cd {{ component_dir }} && {% endif %}uv sync --frozen    # install / sync dependencies

just fix       # ruff format + ruff check --fix — run before committing
just check     # everything CI runs
```

`just check` is `just static test`, i.e. `lint` (ruff format --check, ruff check), `typecheck` (pyright) and `test` (pytest with coverage). Each can be run alone.

| Gate | What it enforces |
|---|---|
| Format | `ruff format --check` — no auto-fix in CI |
| Lint | ruff with `select = ["ALL"]`; the ignore list is in `pyproject.toml` |
| Typecheck | pyright strict — no implicit `Any`, public functions annotated |
| Tests | pytest with branch coverage; fails below 80% |

CI runs `just check` on every push and pull request.
{%- if platform in ('forgejo', 'both') %}

**CI secret (Forgejo).** The runner needs a `GH_PAT` Actions secret — a GitHub personal access token that can read public repositories (no scopes required) — so the `setup-just` and `setup-uv` actions can download their tools from github.com. A user- or organisation-level secret covers every repository of that owner; otherwise add it to this repository under **Settings → Actions → Secrets**.
{%- if is_service %} CD logs in to the container registry with the built-in `GITHUB_TOKEN` and `packages: write`, so it needs no further secret.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

### Pre-commit hooks
{% if hooks == 'none' %}
No pre-commit hooks are configured for this project.
{%- else %}
Install once after cloning with `pre-commit install`; run all of them with `pre-commit run --all-files`.

| Hook | What it checks |
|------|---------------|
| `trailing-whitespace` | No trailing spaces |
| `end-of-file-fixer` | Files end with exactly one newline |
| `check-yaml` | Valid YAML syntax |
| `check-added-large-files` | No files over 500 kB |
| `detect-secrets` | No secrets or credentials |
| `no-commit-to-main` | No commits directly on `main`/`master` |
{%- if hooks == 'python' %}
| `fix` | `just fix` — ruff format and lint with auto-fix |
| `typecheck` | `just typecheck` — pyright |
{%- endif %}

`detect-secrets` runs without a baseline file: every finding blocks the commit. Mark a false positive on its line:

```python
EXAMPLE_KEY = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"  # pragma: allowlist secret
```

If a project accumulates false positives that cannot be annotated inline (generated files, fixtures full of fake keys), switch to a baseline: run `detect-secrets scan > .secrets.baseline`, commit the file, and add `args: ["--baseline", ".secrets.baseline"]` to the hook in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.
{%- endif %}

### Definition of done

A change is ready to merge when:

- [ ] `just check` passes locally and CI is green on the PR
- [ ] New behaviour has test coverage
- [ ] `docs/` is updated where the change affects documented behaviour
- [ ] Any new architectural decision is recorded in `docs/adr/`
- [ ] The PR body says how the change was verified and what was not

## Releases

Releases are cut from `main` with `just release vX.Y.Z` ([semver](https://semver.org/)), on the human's request; the procedure is the `release` skill.

The recipe refuses to run unless it is on a clean `main` that matches `origin/main`. It generates the release notes from the conventional commits since the last tag (git-cliff, configured in `cliff.toml`) and creates the tag and the release page on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %}. The tag triggers the CD workflow{% if is_service %}, which builds and pushes the Docker image{% endif %}.

There is no `CHANGELOG.md`: the release pages are the changelog (`just releases`), so a release needs no commit and no PR.
